<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Spanish Trinidad</h1><p class="st-meta">1592 CE – 1797 CE · capital San José de Oruña</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Spain's long-neglected colony on Trinidad, transformed in its last years by French Catholic planters before British conquest in 1797.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> sighted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> in 1498, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> made no lasting settlement until 1592, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-berrio">Antonio de Berrío</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-jose-de-oruna">San José de Oruña</a> as a base for searching for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-dorado">El Dorado</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco">Orinoco</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-raleigh">Walter Raleigh</a> burnt it in 1595.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>For nearly two centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> was a poor, underpopulated outpost with a few Spanish settlers, missions and indigenous communities. The capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-of-spain">Port of Spain</a> in 1757. In 1783 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cedula-of-population">Cedula of Population</a> offered land to Catholic settlers, drawing French planters and enslaved Africans from the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and creating a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and cocoa economy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1797 a British force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralph-abercromby">Ralph Abercromby</a> captured the island, and Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-chacon">José María Chacón</a> surrendered. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-amiens">Treaty of Amiens</a> in 1802, and it became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-trinidad">Colony of Trinidad</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1592 CE – 1797 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.38%;width:4.08%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1592 CE – 1797 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>San José de Oruña</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Antonio de Berrío, José María Chacón</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish, French Creole</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/trinidad-and-tobago">Trinidad and Tobago</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Province of Trinidad</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1694&amp;focus=spanish-trinidad" aria-label="Spanish Trinidad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2992 1787 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Spanish Trinidad lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3312 2043L4910 989M3312 2043L4997 651M3312 2043L3305 2036"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0M4997 651h0M3305 2036h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3312 2043h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3312 2043h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1694&amp;focus=spanish-trinidad">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
